%3D&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_X9A5kH_gCEiv-3r2UIhrR7S_6aN8cJ29Lx6clPFZYab2Z1x0ePVyB=
wbUvIlxmU1fCNlSgiTpjtG_uzRY9NyqjZ2ja_Q&_hsmi=3D397757708 ) The Wall Street =
Journal. This has led some companies to offer it the way others provide pro=
tein bars and kombucha on tap.

Why? And also, how?

One study found (https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/the-leading-edge/2=
02506/the-hidden-healing-power-of-nicotine?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_X9A5kH_gCEiv-3=
r2UIhrR7S_6aN8cJ29Lx6clPFZYab2Z1x0ePVyBwbUvIlxmU1fCNlSgiTpjtG_uzRY9NyqjZ2ja=
_Q&_hsmi=3D397757708 ) that nicotine can have brain-boosting benefits, impr=
oving memory, attention, and cognitive processing. Alex Cohen, founder of a=
n AI health-tech startup, told WSJ that, as someone with ADHD, it helps him=
 focus.

The preferred method of delivery is a flavored pouch, such as those produce=
d by startups Lucy Nicotine and Sesh, that people tuck between their gums a=
nd teeth.

Palantir Technologies currently has a vending machine from each brand in it=
s Washington, D.C., offices, with pouches free for anyone 21 and up.

Is it dangerous?

Compared to smoking cigarettes =E2=80=94 the leading risk factor for lung c=
ancer =E2=80=94 no.

But nicotine is still an addictive substance with potential negative health=
 outcomes (https://www.mdanderson.org/cancerwise/what-to-know-about-nicotin=
e-pouches.h00-159773289.html?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_X9A5kH_gCEiv-3r2UIhrR7S_6aN8=
cJ29Lx6clPFZYab2Z1x0ePVyBwbUvIlxmU1fCNlSgiTpjtG_uzRY9NyqjZ2ja_Q&_hsmi=3D397=
757708 ) . A Swedish dentist told the BBC about some gnarly gum lesions (ht=
tps://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c9395ywzg73o?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_X9A5kH_gCEiv=
-3r2UIhrR7S_6aN8cJ29Lx6clPFZYab2Z1x0ePVyBwbUvIlxmU1fCNlSgiTpjtG_uzRY9NyqjZ2=
ja_Q&_hsmi=3D397757708 ) he'd seen among pouch users that were so deep, the=
 roots of nearby teeth were visible.

There's also concern that pouch users might seek out nicotine in other, mor=
e harmful forms, including cigarettes.

And perhaps it's mildly dystopian to think about companies supplying a habi=
t-forming substance that makes workers more productive=E2=80=A6 until you r=
emember that=E2=80=99s also coffee.

Speaking of coffee=E2=80=A6

Forty-four percent of US companies offer free snacks, a key motivator (http=
s://www.inc.com/kit-eaton/yes-food-perks-at-work-do-motivate-employees-this=
-study-says/91244821?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_X9A5kH_gCEiv-3r2UIhrR7S_6aN8cJ29Lx6c=
lPFZYab2Z1x0ePVyBwbUvIlxmU1fCNlSgiTpjtG_uzRY9NyqjZ2ja_Q&_hsmi=3D397757708 )=
 for workers. Yet a tax law that allowed companies to deduct the cost of fr=
ee food and beverages expired on Dec. 31, 2025. The change is expected to r=
aise an additional $32B in taxes on employers through 2034.

It=E2=80=99s currently unclear if companies will pull back or not, per (htt=
ps://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-07-12/trump-tax-law-quietly-takes=
-aim-at-popular-perk-office-snacks?embedded-checkout=3Dtrue&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqt=
z-_X9A5kH_gCEiv-3r2UIhrR7S_6aN8cJ29Lx6clPFZYab2Z1x0ePVyBwbUvIlxmU1fCNlSgiTp=
jtG_uzRY9NyqjZ2ja_Q&_hsmi=3D397757708 ) Bloomberg.

But it actually may not matter what perks employers provide. Forbes predict=
s (https://www.forbes.com/sites/bryanrobinson/2025/12/29/2026-work-trends-1=
0-experts--predict-the-future-of-work/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-_X9A5kH_gCEiv-3r2UI=
hrR7S_6aN8cJ29Lx6clPFZYab2Z1x0ePVyBwbUvIlxmU1fCNlSgiTpjtG_uzRY9NyqjZ2ja_Q&_=
hsmi=3D397757708 ) that in 2026, one of the hottest office perks will be th=
e ability to not go there, thanks to the rise =E2=80=94 and popularity =E2=
=80=94 of remote and hybrid roles.

Those workers will have to provide their own bananas and nicotine fridges.

